  "response": "## `svg_with_embedded_raster` finding for `src/assets/partner-logo.svg`\n\n**Finding:** `crime_00035`, severity high, confidence 0.95\n\nFrom the evidence:\n- **Number of embedded rasters: 2** (`2 <image href=\"data:image/…;base64,…\"> occurrences`)\n- **MIME types: `image/jpeg`, `image/png`**\n- SVG byte size: 592 bytes, including the base64 overhead\n\n### What's in the file\n\n```\n<image x=\"0\"   y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" href=\"data:image/png;base64,…\"/>        ← left mark (PNG)\n<image x=\"100\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" xlink:href=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,…\"/> ← right mark (JPEG)\n```\n\nThere are no vector shapes at all. The file is an SVG that only wraps two bitmaps. Two more problems turned up while reading it:\n- Both payloads are **1×1-pixel** images stretched to 80×80. The blurring the finding warns about is at its worst here.\n- The JPEG uses `xlink:href`, but the root element never declares `xmlns:xlink`. That makes the file invalid XML: strict parsers such as SVGO, some bundlers, and `<object>` embedding will reject it. The PNG uses plain SVG2 `href`, so the file mixes the two styles.\n\n### Remediation steps\n\n**1. Re-author the PNG slot (x=0, 80×80) as vector paths.** A PNG at logo-mark size is almost always a flat-colour mark exported by mistake. Ask the partner for their vector source (an SVG, AI or EPS from their brand kit), or re-export it from the design tool with the layer kept as vector. Then add it as a `<g>` with `<path>`/`<rect>` elements, using `transform=\"translate(0 0)\"` and its viewBox scaled to 80×80. Only fall back to tracing it yourself (Illustrator Image Trace, Inkscape Trace Bitmap, potrace) if no vector source exists, and get partner sign-off for traced brand marks.\n\n**2. Split the JPEG slot (x=100, 80×80) out to a standalone raster asset**, unless it turns out to be flat artwork too. JPEG is a strong sign of photographic or gradient-heavy content, which vectors can't represent faithfully. Create:\n- `src/assets/partner-logo-photo.webp`, exported at 160×160 and 240×240 (2× and 3× of the 80px slot) from the original high-resolution source. Don't upscale the 1×1 payload.\n- Optionally `src/assets/partner-logo-photo.png` as a fallback if you need to support older targets.\n\nIf the partner confirms the JPEG slot is also flat artwork, treat it like step 1 and make the whole SVG pure vector.\n\n**3. Rewrite `partner-logo.svg`.** It keeps its path and viewBox, so callers don't change:\n\n```svg\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"200\" height=\"80\" viewBox=\"0 0 200 80\" role=\"img\">\n  <title>Partner logo</title>\n  <g id=\"mark\"><!-- vector paths re-authored from the former PNG slot --></g>\n  <image x=\"100\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" href=\"partner-logo-photo.webp\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\nThis uses plain `href`, so `xlink` is no longer needed and the namespace bug goes away. In the fully-vector case, the `<image>` line becomes a second `<g>` of paths.\n\n**4. Watch the external-reference caveat.** An SVG loaded through `<img src>` or CSS `background-image` will **not** load external resources, so the WebP slot would render blank. If the logo is used that way, keep the SVG pure vector and render the raster part as a sibling element in the component (`<img src={partnerLogoSvg}>` next to `<img src={partnerPhotoWebp} srcset=\"…160w, …240w\">`). Use the referenced-`<image>` form only for inline SVG or `<object>`. A grep of `src/` found no references to `partner-logo`, so decide this when the logo is wired up.\n\n**5. Verify.** Re-run `crimes scan --format json` and confirm `svg_with_embedded_raster::src/assets/partner-logo.svg::` is gone. Also run `svgo --dry-run` or `xmllint` to confirm the SVG parses, and check visually at 1×, 2× and 4× that the vector mark is sharp.\n\n### Files after the refactor\n- `src/assets/partner-logo.svg`: kept, vector geometry only, with no `data:` URIs\n- `src/assets/partner-logo-photo.webp` (plus a `.png` fallback if needed): new, only if the JPEG slot really is photographic",
